#lego-legacy-route 1
# LEGO Chess, from its boot into its throne room and onto its Quick Game screen: Space skips
# Krisalis' logo and the intro, a player named Ada is made at the New Player form, the chess board
# in the throne room is clicked, and the window is closed (#210, #157). #158 plays a game from here.
#
# Replay it with a fresh `--data-root <empty dir>`, never beside the rip. It reaches the New Player
# form only when no profile is saved, and it saves one: the game writes profiles\prof1.lcp and
# profiles\1save.lcs for the player it makes, which beside the rip would land among the live saves
# in game-data/chess/profile, and the next replay would boot past the form.
#
# The logo plays through, and Space skips Krisalis' logo as in skip-krisalis.route: down at flip
# 290 and up at the 300th poll after it, and the intro opens after flip 294. Space goes down again
# at flip 300, as the intro plays: the game ends it, waits for Space to come up (0x0040D220),
# presenting nothing, and Space comes up at the 300th poll after the flip. The movie player clears
# the screen twice, flips 301 and 302, and the game draws its bumper through Direct3D 2, flips 303 and
# 304. While the bumper stands it loads its rooms' files and decodes their first fifteen sounds
# (#153), going on without the one neither pressing ships, sounds\YAHOO.wav; flip 305 is black.
# With no profile saved it asks for a new player: flip 306 has the form's buttons and boxes on
# black, and from flip 307 the New Player form stands on parchment between the inkpot and the
# quill, its name box's caret first on at flip 319.
#
# The name is typed a key at a time, each let go before the next goes down. The name box
# (0x00448320) takes a character once a frame, from the WM_CHAR its key types (#157), when it is
# not the one it took last and at least 50 ms after it, so a key down and up inside one frame, or
# two keys, would lose one. Left Shift goes down at flip 320 and A at 321, and the box shows A from
# flip 322; Shift is let go at 325, so D at 328 shows Ad from 329, and A at 334 Ada from 335.
#
# The pointer goes to the tick at flip 345, and the left button goes down at flip 350: the game
# waits for it to come up, presenting nothing, which it does at the 300th poll after the flip. The
# game saves the player and lists Ada under "Who's playing?" from flip 352. The tick, pressed again
# at flip 390 and let go at its 300th poll, takes her in: the game decodes twelve more sounds, and
# the throne room presents from flip 392, the camera flying in to the King as he starts speaking.
#
# The room's camera turns after the pointer, so what is under a pointer held still changes as it
# turns: at (385,285) the chess board stays under it once the camera has turned, and the game names
# it "Quick game". The pointer goes there at flip 405, and the button goes down at flip 430, the
# camera still, and up at 431, since the game does not wait for it here. The Quick Game screen
# presents whole from flip 432, white played by a person and black by the computer, a normal game
# at a difficulty of 25, its tick starting the game. The window is closed at flip 470, and the
# game leaves.
flip 290 key Space down
flip 290 poll 300 key Space up
flip 300 key Space down
flip 300 poll 300 key Space up
flip 320 key LeftShift down
flip 321 key A down
flip 324 key A up
flip 325 key LeftShift up
flip 328 key D down
flip 331 key D up
flip 334 key A down
flip 337 key A up
flip 345 move 576 420
flip 350 down
flip 350 poll 300 up
flip 390 down
flip 390 poll 300 up
flip 405 move 385 285
flip 430 down
flip 431 up
flip 470 close
